      sectionTitle: "Wakalah (Agency) / Types of Wakalah",
      title: "Wakalah (Agency): Definition, Types, and General Rules",
      overview: "Defines Wakalah, catalogs its recognized types, and details what acts can and cannot be delegated to an agent, plus the agent's duties, liabilities, and how a Wakalah contract ends.",
      simpleExplanation: "Wakalah just means appointing someone to act on your behalf - like a trustee looking after your interests. It's fully backed by the Prophet's (pbuh) own practice (he once delegated buying a goat to a companion). You can delegate almost any normal business task (buying, selling, lending, guaranteeing, payments) but NOT personal religious/legal acts you must do yourself (like praying, giving testimony, or taking an oath). The agent must follow instructions carefully, can't secretly sell his own property to the person he's representing without disclosing it, and generally can't pass the job to someone else without permission. If the agent messes up through negligence, he can be held liable.",
      academicExplanation: "'Wakalah' literally means looking after, taking custody, or applying skill/remedy on behalf of others - the word 'Tawkeel' derives from it, meaning to appoint someone to take charge or delegate a job. Wakalah is also a RESPONSIBILITY - a Wakil (agent) must discharge his duty the way a trustee discharges Amanah. Five recognized types: Wakil-bil-Kusoomah (taking up disputes/cases on the principal's behalf), Wakil-bil-Taqazi al Dayn (receiving debt), Wakil-bil-Qabaza al Dayn (possession of debt), Wakil-bil-Bai' (trading agency), Wakil-bil-Shira (purchase agency). Agency is proven by Shari'ah texts - the Prophet (pbuh) himself delegated goat-purchasing to Companion 'Urwah al Barqi; Caliph Ali and other Companions delegated business to others. The subject matter/act to be performed must be known/defined - for a purchase agency, the genus, kind, quality, and other necessary attributes of the commodity must be specified. Agency is NOT permissible for acts prohibited by Shari'ah (theft, property usurpation, Riba-based business) or acts of disobedience. An agency-eligible act must ADMIT REPRESENTATION - agency for prayer, fasting, giving evidence, or taking an oath is not permissible, since these must be performed by the principal personally (e.g. an eyewitness must give testimony himself, not delegate it). Acts for which agency CAN be invoked: sale/purchase, letting/hiring, borrowing/lending, debt assignment, guarantee, pledge, gifts, bailment, payments, marriage/divorce, litigation/relinquishment, and admission/acknowledgement of rights. An agency contract may be SPECIFIC (e.g. selling a particular asset at a given price/instruction) or GENERAL (e.g. purchasing certain kinds of goods as and when asked) - even in a general contract, the job's nature must be clearly defined to avoid disputes. Rights/liabilities attributed to the agent include: delivery of subject matter, claiming price, exercising Khiyar al 'Aib/inspection options, and returning goods on reclaim - the agent is responsible for making payment/receiving goods on the principal's behalf, can demand price from a purchaser (in a sale), and can be SUED for non-performance per the agency agreement. Certain contracts, however, are attributed explicitly to the PRINCIPAL only - marriage, divorce, and murder settlement. The agent must perform per the principal's instructions with due care/skill, cannot delegate to another person without the principal's consent, and must avoid conflicts of interest (e.g. cannot sell his own property to the principal without fully disclosing his ownership). Agency can generate THIRD-PARTY liabilities - banks appointing agents should ensure good faith/due diligence, with a contractual clause imposing liability for negligence. An agent's action on the principal's behalf is deemed the PRINCIPAL's own action; per the preferred view (especially Shafi'i and Hanbali), ownership of a commodity purchased by an agent transfers DIRECTLY from seller to principal, without first passing through the agent's ownership. A person acting WITHOUT proper authority is termed Fuduli - the preferred view is that such unauthorized contracts are VALID subject to the principal's subsequent RATIFICATION. A Wakalah contract ends via: mutual agreement, unilateral termination, discharge of obligation, destruction of the subject matter, or death/loss of legal capacity. Wakalah is used by IFIs across almost all modes (Murabaha, Salam, Istisna'a, Ijarah, Diminishing Musharakah) and activities (L/Cs, bill payment/collection, fund management, securitization). Wakalah can be COMMUTATIVE (fee-based) or NONCOMMUTATIVE (no fee) - Islamic banks mostly do NOT pay clients acting as their purchase/sale agents, but normally DO charge fees for agency services they themselves render on clients' behalf (e.g. Wakalatul Istismar fund management).",
      examExplanation: "Wakalah = agency, a trust-based responsibility. 5 types: Wakil-bil-Kusoomah (disputes), Wakil-bil-Taqazi/Qabaza al Dayn (debt receipt/possession), Wakil-bil-Bai' (trading), Wakil-bil-Shira (purchase). Agency requires the task to be known/defined and must ADMIT REPRESENTATION - personal religious/legal acts (prayer, testimony, oath) CANNOT be delegated; business acts (sale, hire, borrowing, guarantee, pledge, payments) CAN. Specific agency (one asset/task) vs general agency (recurring category). Agent's action = principal's action (per Shafi'i/Hanbali, ownership transfers DIRECTLY seller-to-principal, bypassing the agent). Fuduli (unauthorized agent) contracts are VALID subject to principal's ratification (preferred view). Wakalah ends via: mutual agreement, unilateral termination, discharge, destruction of subject matter, or death/incapacity. Used across virtually all IFI modes; can be fee-based (commutative) or free (noncommutative) - banks typically charge for THEIR agency services rendered, but don't pay clients acting as the bank's purchase/sale agent.",
