      simpleExplanation: "Instead of sharing profit like in a Mudarabah, a bank can simply act as a paid agent managing investor money - charging a flat fee (either a lump sum or a percentage of the fund's value each year), no matter how the investments actually perform. This fee structure has to be decided and clearly disclosed BEFORE the fund launches, typically in the fund's prospectus, and everyone who invests is presumed to accept those disclosed terms.",
      academicExplanation: "Islamic financial institutions can manage investor funds on the basis of Wakalatul Istismar (investment agency services). For their services, banks can charge a PRE-AGREED fee, IRRESPECTIVE of the relevant portfolio's profit or loss - this fee can be a lump sum, or a monthly/annual remuneration as a percentage of investment amount or net asset value (NAV) (e.g. an agreement that management gets 2-3% of the fund's NAV at each financial year end). It is necessary to determine the specific fee-calculation method BEFORE the fund launches - practically done by disclosing the management fee basis in the fund's prospectus; whoever subscribes to the fund is presumed to agree with the prospectus's terms, so the fee-payment manner is taken as agreed upon by all subscribers.",
