  chapterSummary: "Chapter 13 covers the accessory contracts that make Islamic finance's major modes operationally possible. Wakalah (agency) underlies nearly every Islamic bank transaction, from MPO to Ijarah to fund management (Wakalatul Istismar), with clear rules on what can be delegated and how an agent's actions bind the principal. Tawarruq generates liquidity through a credit-purchase-then-cash-resale structure, permissible across all four schools when resold to a genuine third party (never back to the original seller, which would be prohibited Bai' al 'Inah) - though scholars strongly caution against its widespread, routine use. Ju'alah's reward-for-result structure uniquely suits tasks with unspecifiable work or uncertain outcomes (debt recovery, lost-property recovery), distinguishing it sharply from Ijarah's requirement of precisely specified work. Bai' al Istijrar rounds out the chapter as a flexible, ordinary repeat-supply arrangement rather than a distinct mode.",
